# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RAW RENDER FRAMES AND RUN WORKING DATA — never committed.
#
# Added 2026-08-28 after an over-broad `git add` swept 867 raw .rgb frames into
# three commits and the owner had to authorise a history rewrite to remove them.
# THE EVIDENCE IS THE PNG CONTACT SHEET, NOT THE FRAMES IT WAS MADE FROM.
# A run's evidence/*.png is committed and belongs in history; the raw frames,
# object files and golden staging dirs are regenerable working data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.rgb
runs/**/renders/
runs/**/renders-baseline/
runs/**/scratch/
runs/**/obj/
runs/**/golden-work/
runs/**/golden-verify/
# scratch reel output, wherever a subject dir lands
**/scratch-reel/
tools/reel/exp/

# Nested clones an agent made inside a run work dir. They were committed as
# GITLINKS with no .gitmodules entry, so every CI checkout ran
# `git submodule foreach` against them and exited 128 -- a warning on every
# single job. They are working clones of sibling repos, never our content.
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260829-0525-zixxtrixx-v9-cel-main-art-pass/work/Upheaval/
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260829-0525-zixxtrixx-v9-cel-main-art-pass/work/zhaozhou-v9/
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260829-2226-zixxtrixx-v10-lighting-spring-repair/work/Upheaval-v10/
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260829-2226-zixxtrixx-v10-lighting-spring-repair/work/zhaozhou-v10/
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260830-0329-creature-authoring-blueprint/work/Upheaval-blueprint/
runs/CLAUDE-RUNS/RUN-20260830-0329-creature-authoring-blueprint/work/zhaozhou-blueprint/
fit-round2.log

# Quartus RAW map reports. The composed island's is 2.2 MB and grows with the
# design, so it trips the >2 MiB commit hook and leaves the tree permanently
# dirty -- a tracked file that can never be committed again is worse than an
# untracked one. The NUMBERS are committed: reports/synthesis/zhao_block_fit.json
# carries the row, *.map.summary and *.fit.summary carry the harvested totals,
# and *.setup.rpt carries the paths. Only the multi-megabyte dump is dropped.
# Smaller blocks' map.rpt files stay tracked; this rule is the size, not the kind.
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/zhao_texture_island_top.map.rpt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRANSIENT FIT CONSOLE LOGS, untracked 2026-09-07
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 45 of these were committed, 37 MB of them, one per block fit ever run. They
# are the console spew of `run_block_fit.ps1` -- the same status lines it
# already parses into `reports/synthesis/zhao_block_fit.json` and the reports
# it harvests into `reports/synthesis/blockpaths/`. THE EVIDENCE IS THE ROW AND
# THE HARVESTED REPORT, not the terminal output they were read from. Same rule
# as `*.rgb` above: the contact sheet, not the frames.
#
# The immediate cause was worse than clutter. A pull could not proceed --
# "unable to unlink old 'fit-pairtn.log': Invalid argument" -- because the file
# was HELD OPEN by a Tee-Object in a running fit, and the rebase that failed
# left the tree mid-rebase with an autostash. A tracked file that a background
# process writes for an hour is a tracked file that will block a merge at the
# worst moment, and the worst moment turned out to be while pulling owner
# instructions.
#
# WHAT NOW REMOVES THEM, because an ignore rule that hides waste is not a rule
# that removes it (CLAUDE.md, the 952 GB incident): nothing yet, deliberately.
# They are 37 MB of plain text in the repo root, not 33 GB of raw frames, and
# they are named per run so they neither grow without bound nor collide. If
# they ever do become a disk problem the answer is the existing
# `tools/maintenance/purge_render_intermediates.py`, which already spares
# anything touched in 48 h. Recorded so the next person does not have to
# rediscover that the question was asked.
fit-*.log

# Quartus per-block MAP reports: 2.3 MB each of synthesis-stage node dumps.
# The evidence is the fit summary, the setup report's gating path and
# reports/synthesis/worst_path_index.json -- not the node list they were
# derived from. Same reasoning as *.rgb: the contact sheet, not the frames.
# Quartus RAW STAGE STDOUT, untracked 2026-09-25 (EDGECLOSE).
# `zhao_console_core@edgeclose.quartus_map.log` is 208 MB and GitHub refuses a
# file over 100 MB, so the push was REJECTED and that is how this was found --
# not by anyone noticing the size.
#
# It is the same class as `fit-*.log` above, which the 2026-09-07 note already
# untracked for the same reason: raw console spew whose useful content is
# already harvested. The naming simply missed it, because run_block_fit.ps1
# writes these into blockpaths/ rather than the repo root.
#
# WHAT IS KEPT AND IS THE EVIDENCE: `*.map.summary` (546 bytes, and it carries
# the register/memory/DSP/virtual-pin totals), `*.quartus_fit.log` (21 KB, and
# it carries the Error 170011 / Error 11802 pair that says WHY a fit failed),
# `*.sources.sha256`, and the row in zhao_block_fit.json. The MAP log is a node
# dump; nothing has ever been read out of it that the summary does not state.
# NOTE the fit log is deliberately NOT ignored -- it is small and it is the
# only place the failure reason appears in words.
#
# WHAT NOW REMOVES THEM, because an ignore rule that hides waste is not a rule
# that removes it (CLAUDE.md, the 952 GB incident): the filename is keyed on
# `<module>@<label>`, so re-running the same row OVERWRITES it rather than
# accumulating, and `tools/maintenance/purge_render_intermediates.py` already
# sweeps anything untouched for 48 h. Asked and answered, so the next person
# does not have to rediscover the question.
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.quartus_map.log
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.map.rpt
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.fit.rpt
# Setup/hold reports too: 2-6 MB each, 50 tracked, ~200 MB of blockpaths.
# What they are READ for is now distilled into worst_path_index.json by
# tools/quartus/worst_path_index.py -- the gating path, the port-vs-internal
# census, what the port boundary is actually worth, and the deepest data-delay
# cone with its clock skew. Those are the questions that have been asked of
# these reports, and the index survives the next fit overwriting them.
#
# WHAT THIS LOSES, stated rather than discovered later: the full per-path
# distribution, for a question nobody has asked yet. If one arises, re-run the
# fit -- the reports were never durable anyway, since the next fit of the same
# module overwrites them in place.
# Test FAILURE artefacts. `captures/failures/` is where a failing test writes
# what it saw; it is output, not evidence, and it arrives in a commit only
# because someone ran `git add -A` while a test was red. Same reasoning as
# *.rgb: keep the report, not the intermediate that produced it.
captures/failures/

reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.setup.rpt
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.hold.rpt
reports/synthesis/blockpaths/*.sta.rpt

